    12	
    13	# Design analysis for the D11 variant
    14	# -----------------------------------
    15	# a) Concrete behavior and trace
    16	# - Keep the original palette mechanic: a 2xN or Nx2 legend sits on one border and
    17	#   maps border colors to fill colors.
    18	# - Replace the original "scattered hollow sprites" layout with one mandatory nested
    19	#   group: a large hollow rectangular shell contains a smaller hollow shell, and a
    20	#   second same-colored nested sprite may sit beside it inside the same outer cavity.
    21	# - The large shell's empty interior is split into two visually distinct enclosed bg
    22	#   regions by the inner hollow shell:
    23	#   1. an annulus between the outer border and the inner shell,
    24	#   2. the inner shell's own hole.
    25	# - The output fills the annulus using the outer shell's palette mapping. The inner
    26	#   hole is then filled only if the inner-shell color also appears as a palette key.
    27	#   If the inner-shell color is a distractor, its hole stays bg.
    28	# - Small trace:
    29	#   original logic on a nested input would inspect the annulus bg component, see both
    30	#   the outer color and the inner color on its boundary, and might choose by raw
    31	#   adjacency frequency. The variant instead asks: which adjacent border actually
    32	#   encloses this bg region as its parent shell? For the annulus, that parent is the
    33	#   outer shell; for the inner hole, it is the inner shell.
    34	#
    35	# b) Complexity calibration
    36	# - The main signal is still one nested hollow shell, but the optional extra nested
    37	#   same-colored sprite is necessary so the annulus can touch the inner color often
    38	#   enough to defeat the original dominant-adjacency heuristic.
    39	# - The palette still has 2-4 entries, so there can be unused mappings; this preserves
    40	#   the original "legend contains extra keys" feel without adding unrelated complexity.
    41	# - The inner shell has two repeated modes across samples: mapped or distractor. That
    42	#   branch is small enough to infer and large enough to be visible.
    43	#
    44	# c) Implicit-assumption audit of the original main()
    45	# - The original `_fill_enclosed_bg_regions` assumes each non-boundary bg component is
    46	#   adjacent to one dominant border color. This was harmless when sprites were
    47	#   separated; it becomes unsafe once an outer hole is split by an inner sprite.
    48	# - The original code also updates the grid in place while using the current grid as
    49	#   evidence. In the nested case, processing order should not change which shell owns a
    50	#   region, so the new implementation reasons from an immutable snapshot and writes to a
    51	#   separate output array.
    52	# - Palette detection and bg-color detection do not depend on the old D11 geometry, so
    53	#   they remain intact.
    54	#
    55	# d) Placement legality
    56	# - The generator reserves a 1-cell moat between the palette and the outer shell.
    57	# - The outer shell is kept at least 1 cell away from the global boundary so its hole
    58	#   never touches the frame.
    59	# - The inner shell is placed strictly inside the outer hole with at least 1 bg cell of
    60	#   clearance from the outer border, guaranteeing a visible annulus.
    61	# - The generator constructs the nested input layout directly, derives the intended
    62	#   output by filling the known annulus and inner-hole regions, and finally checks
    63	#   `main(input) == output`.
    64	#
    65	# e) Visual observability
    66	# - In every generated sample there is at least one enclosed bg region adjacent to two
    67	#   different colors, and the inner color appears multiple times inside the outer
    68	#   cavity. That makes the nesting signature visible and also prevents regression back
    69	#   to the old adjacency-based behavior.
    70	# - The outer fill stops cleanly at the inner shell border, so the containment relation
    71	#   is readable by eye.
    72	#
    73	# f) Visual cleanliness
    74	# - Only four semantic structures appear in the play area: the palette, the outer
    75	#   shell, the inner hollow shell, and one small same-colored nested sprite. No extra
    76	#   markers are added beyond what is needed to expose the containment rule.
    77	# - The outer shell stays rectangular for readability; the smaller shell is slightly
    78	#   tabbed so its presence is easy to see even when the annulus wraps around it.
    79	#
    80	# g) Color-change inferability
    81	# - Colors never change by numeric ordering or hidden IDs. All fills are justified only
    82	#   by the visible edge palette and the shell color that encloses the region.
    83	# - The inner shell's distractor mode is also visible: its color is absent from the
    84	#   palette keys, so its hole staying bg is explainable from the examples.
    85	#
    86	# 9) New solver assumptions to check against the generator
    87	# - The solver assumes each relevant shell is a single connected non-bg component. The
    88	#   generator uses simple rectangular rings, so that always holds.
    89	# - The solver chooses the smallest enclosing adjacent component by bbox area. The
    90	#   generator creates proper nesting, so the immediate parent shell is always the
